# The build context for the DEVCONTAINER image, and only for it.
#
# WHY THERE IS A SECOND ALLOWLIST AT ALL. This image used to build from
# `.devcontainer/` as its context, which was enough while everything it installed
# was a download pinned by a script sitting in that directory. It is not enough
# any more: gg's eleven program-language toolchains are pinned by the REPOSITORY —
# each arm's own `packages/gg-sandbox-*/<lang>-version.sh`, installed by
# `scripts/ci/install-gg-toolchains.sh` — and `crates/gg/build.rs` reflects all
# eleven signature catalogues as a step of building the crate, so a container that
# does not have them cannot `cargo build --workspace` at all. Baking them into the
# image therefore means the image build has to be able to READ the repository, so
# the context moved up to the repository root (see `docker-compose.yml`'s
# `context: ..`).
#
# WHY THAT SLICE IS NOT SIMPLY ADDED TO THE ROOT `.dockerignore`. Read that file's
# opening paragraph: it is an allowlist rather than a list of exclusions precisely
# so "a change to anything cargo does NOT compile — the frontend, the docs, test
# cases, k8s manifests, models, this devcontainer — can never invalidate a build
# layer". Seven Dockerfiles built from that context do `COPY . .` (the shared
# asset-tooling builder that compiles ~20 Rust binaries, the adversarial and
# performance run images, the web console image and all three service targets), so
# re-including `/.devcontainer` there would mean editing this comment recompiled
# the asset tooling from scratch. The root file already refuses to admit
# `scripts/` as a whole for exactly that reason. A per-dockerfile ignore gets the
# devcontainer the slice it needs with zero blast radius: the sibling Dockerfiles
# keep reading the root allowlist, unchanged and unaffected.
#
# HOW DOCKER FINDS THIS FILE. BuildKit looks for `<dockerfile-path>.dockerignore`
# beside the Dockerfile and uses it INSTEAD OF the context root's when it exists —
# so this file wins for `.devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile` and applies to nothing
# else. `docker compose build` honours the same rule, which is what matters here
# because compose is how the Dev Containers extension builds this image; verified by
# moving this file aside, at which point the compose build dies with
# `"/.devcontainer/...": not found` on the first COPY it resolves, and putting it
# back, at which point it builds.
#
# THIS REQUIRES BUILDKIT. The classic builder (`DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0`) does not know
# about per-dockerfile ignore files: it silently falls back to the root allowlist and
# stops at the first COPY with `COPY failed: file not found in build context or
# excluded by .dockerignore`. Docker has defaulted to BuildKit since 23 and the
# devcontainer's own `docker` client ships `buildx`, so this is a statement of a
# requirement rather than a caveat — if a build of this image ever reports a missing
# `.devcontainer/...` file, check that first.
#
# AND IT IS A REQUIREMENT THIS REPOSITORY DOES NOT MEET ON EVERY SUPPORTED HOST.
# `docker-compose.nixos.yml` and `.env.podman` are committed, and `README.md` tells a
# NixOS user to copy them, so ROOTLESS PODMAN is a configuration somebody is expected
# to build this image with. Podman does not go through BuildKit: Buildah resolves an
# ignore file from the CONTEXT directory — `$CONTEXT/.containerignore`, then
# `$CONTEXT/.dockerignore` — with `--ignorefile` as the only override, and implements
# no sibling-to-the-Dockerfile rule. On such a host the ROOT allowlist is what applies
# to this build, and it never admits `/.devcontainer`, so the first COPY fails with
# "no such file or directory". Not verified here, because there is no podman in this
# container to verify it with; it is stated as the thing to check first rather than as
# a measurement.
#
# There is no mechanism both builders honour, which is why this is documented instead
# of solved: a root `.containerignore` would be preferred by Buildah for EVERY image
# built from this context, which is precisely the blast radius the paragraph above
# refuses. The workaround is one flag, and it has to go on a `podman build` rather than
# on compose:
#
#     podman build --ignorefile .devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile.dockerignore \
#       -f .devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile -t <the tag compose expects> .
#
# `.devcontainer/README.md`'s Podman section says the same thing where a person looking
# for it would actually be standing.

# Ignore everything by default…
*

# …then re-include the four things this image's COPY instructions read.

# The devcontainer's own scripts and shell config — the whole directory, because
# every file in it exists to be copied into the image and enumerating them here
# would be a second list to keep in step with the Dockerfile's COPYs.
!/.devcontainer

# The toolchain installers, and only them, as a GLOB. `install-gg-toolchains.sh`
# runs eight per-arm installers by that name and sources `scripts/ci/lib.sh` beside
# them, so those two patterns are the whole closure — and being a glob rather than a
# list, the half that grows every time an arm is added is covered without an edit.
#
# It is deliberately NOT the whole `scripts/ci` directory, though the root allowlist
# admits it that way for the driver image. The Dockerfile's COPY is written to match
# these same two patterns, and the reason is the 1.9 GB layer it feeds: Docker hashes
# everything a COPY matches into that layer's cache key, so admitting the other twelve
# scripts here would mean an edit to `web-build.sh` or `specs-lint.sh` cost the next
# image build every toolchain in it.
!/scripts/ci/install-*.sh
!/scripts/ci/lib.sh

# The version files those installers source, one per arm, as a GLOB — so adding an
# arm needs no edit here, exactly as it needs no edit in the Dockerfile. The
# pattern admits `packages/gg-sandbox-*/*-version.sh` and nothing else under
# `packages/`: `*` does not cross a `/`, so the arms' sources, their `.build/`
# scratch and the unrelated npm workspaces beside them all stay out. Measured
# whole slice: ~230 kB.
!/packages/gg-sandbox-*/*-version.sh

# `packages/gg-sandbox-rust/rust-version.sh` reads the toolchain channel out of
# this file rather than pinning a second copy of it, so the Rust arm's installer
# (`rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown`) cannot run without it. It is also
# what makes that install cheap: `channel` here equals the `RUST_VERSION` build arg
# in `docker-compose.yml`, so rustup adds a target to the toolchain the image
# already has rather than downloading a second one.
!/rust-toolchain.toml

# Finally, re-exclude the two host-specific files that are deliberately not
# committed (see `.devcontainer/.gitignore`). They would ride in under the
# `!/.devcontainer` re-inclusion above, which would put one developer's SSH-agent
# path and timezone into the context of everyone's build. Nothing COPYs them —
# `docker-compose.local.yml` is read by compose on the HOST, before any of this.
# These come last so they win.
/.devcontainer/docker-compose.local.yml
/.devcontainer/.env
